A||||2||Orlando||Lumpy Idle
Z||000000||Orlando||07-10-99||04:39 PM||||I have an '88 Formula with a fluctuating idle. It "hovers" between 1100 and 750 RPM once the engine is hot. The CEL is working, but has not come on, and there are no codes stored. The plugs are new, so are the PCV valve, wires, cap & rotor. I've taken the car to just about everywhere, Precision Tune, and even (gulp) Pep Boys! Everyone says the condition is normal for "such an old car". By the way its got 80k miles on the clock. ANY help will be greatly appreciated! - email or forum - thanks!
Z||000001||theogre||07-11-99||06:22 PM||||As far as I can tell, this may be the ECM trying to keep emissions low. I've seen this in a number of older GM cars, and no amount of changing things seems to help.<p>In my own 87 4 cyl for example, if I sit idled for a while, like when the jackasses in the CTX yard block the road with a train, the motor starts floating the idle. The only thing I can think of is that the ECM it trying to control the emiisions, or their is a sensor heating up and screwing with the normal control of things.... since most of my sensors are new, I tend to think it's the first item. It's annoying as hell, but there doesn't seem to be a fix so far.<p>In the case of a V6, is could be a vac leak, or very likely, a small EGR leak. Make sure you check for that very well.
Z||000002||Orlando||07-18-99||09:33 AM||||Thanks guys! The culprit was a cracked EGR tube causing a vacuum leak.
